{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791196251,"nonce":"kYGMV04EZ53nil29O5Qq45OU35Y=","message":"Implemented in f8889cb6 (local, not pushed).\n\nWhat it does: a non-emoji CJK character the primary lacks takes the first resolved CJK face that has it, then the symbol face. Faces are loaded in candidate order only until every needed character is covered, and a face that adds no coverage is dropped. Layout substitutes glyph id and advance, so measure, wrap and draw agree. Each CJK face keys the glyph cache on its candidate name, since FontFace::family_name() reports \"Unknown\" for these faces.\n\nChecked on macOS only: drawn vs measured widths within 0.5px for System/Monospace/Serif; offscreen capture of Han, kana, Hangul, full-width punctuation, emoji and symbols in one line, and a wrapped paragraph.\n\nKnown limits:\n- Han unification: Simplified, Traditional, Japanese and Korean share codepoints and the first candidate that has one wins. No locale or lang input exists yet.\n- PingFang is not found by name by the registry on this machine (it lives outside the scanned directories), so macOS currently resolves through Hiragino Sans GB, Hiragino Sans, Apple SD Gothic Neo, then Arial Unicode MS. Simplified-only characters (e.g. 这 长 标) were boxes when Hiragino Sans came first; the list now puts the Simplified face ahead of the Japanese one.\n- Windows and Linux candidate lists are untested.\n- Weight and italic are not carried to the fallback face.\n- The styled-text path does not resolve CJK faces (its layout cache key uses no fallbacks).","files":null}]}